I  hey guys Peter Brown here I'm with Scott  Butterworth who just got a chance to  look at the latest version of Star Fox  for wii u scott i played this back at e3  and i walked away a little bit  unimpressed because nintendo put a lot  of effort into making sure that the  gamepad was utilized to his full extent  and in doing so I kind of divided up the  game a bit like I had to aim with the  gamepad but I could only see where my  ship was going if I looked up at the  television and that created a really odd  experience for me where I felt like one  it wasn't as traditional star fox as the  game looked and to the controls weren't  intuitive enough for me to just jump  right in I'm curious how your experience  was playing as you build I think I'm  feeling maybe a little more optimistic  because I can kind of see where they're  going with it the controls still  function largely the same way I mean it  is still that split you know cockpit  view on one screen and third-person  perspective on the other screen you can  now switch which screen displays which  display so if you want your cockpit view  up on the TV you can do that you can  actually swap so whatever feels more  intuitive to you but yeah ultimately you  are still trying to dart your eyes  between two separate screens in an  effort to kind of get a better idea of  what's actually happening in the game I  do get what they're going for I mean  like if anybody who's played in  traditional starfox game knows that if  you're trying to like shoot a turret on  the ground for example you're also going  to be kind of flying into the ground in  the process because you can only shoot  where your ship is pointed so I think  divorcing those two things allowing your  ship to fly in one direction while you  aim in another direction fundamentally  makes a lot of sense  and in fact almost creates a layer of  skill that that wasn't there before the  only issue is it kind of works better on  paper than it does in practice and I  think that may just reflect the fact  that neither of us had a truly  substantial amount of time to play the  game to really adjust to what's altima  tlie a very new approach to this kind of  game so I'm hoping that with time that  idea will pay off because again I think  the idea is good when I played starfox  for Wii U I didn't notice that the level  design had benefited in any way from the  ability for you to look around while  you're shooting for example sure like it  didn't seem to allow tan intend or  platinum in this case to make the levels  any more different it still looked like  the old starfox levels just with this  added layer that I wasn't used to did  you notice anything where it felt like  this aspect of this level is only  possible now that they've made these  changes clearly certain boss encounters  in particular are designed with this  dual screen approach in mind one of the  alternate routes that you can find leads  to a boss encounter that has you in all  range mode and have to shoot a bunch of  like spider robots off of a tower and  the way that you approach them it  actually makes more sense to sort of be  looking at the screen so you can aim  precisely and shoot down on top of them  is the thing like they're weak point is  on the top of their body so you have to  kind of like fly over them and shoot  down directly on top of them so clearly  that enemy was designed to capitalize on  this particular approach to the gameplay  so they I think they were mine to live  in and I would hope that as you play  you'll find more instances like that  so I think the starfox game that people  want is it's in Star Fox zero somewhere  it's just hopefully we'll be able to  like dig past that initial layer of  potential frustration and learn to it  sort of embrace and enjoy this more sort  of nuanced and demanding gameplay and  once we're able to embrace that we'll be  able to truly enjoy all of the you know  long-standing aspects that of the series  that we've always loved I hope you're  right we'll know in a few weeks starfox  for Wii U comes out in April  you  you

